        "transcriptHtml": "<p class=\"transcript-note\">Automatic transcript. It may contain small recognition errors; the answer key is authoritative.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[13:53]</span> Now turn to section three. You will hear three students discussing the issue of waste deposited in space. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 23. Open carefully to the first part of the discussion and answer questions 21 to 23.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[14:30]</span> Hey, did you manage to go to the talk by Dr Chadwick this morning Andy? I was there. What happened to you Sam? My bike had a puncture.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[14:43]</span> Seriously. Anyway, Ruth, I bet you took some notes. Can you fill me in? Sure.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[14:49]</span> It was all about space junk. Really interesting actually. I mean, I knew about how much rubbish humans are dumping here on planet Earth, but I had no idea how much junk there is flying around in space.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[15:02]</span> Did you know that there are literally millions of pieces of rubbish orbiting the Earth as we speak? Not until now I didn&#x27;t. They reckon that around a hundred tons of very small objects, like mainly dust, drops</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[15:15]</span> on Earth every single day. Yes, that&#x27;s what she said. I thought space junk was all man-made. I can&#x27;t believe they know so accurately how much is actually out there.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[15:28]</span> Do they track and monitor it all the time? Yeah, they do. According to the talk, there are nearly 25,000 objects larger than 10 centimetres in diameter now orbiting the Earth.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[15:40]</span> And what does all this space junk consist of? Isn&#x27;t it all discarded parts of rockets that were either broken or left behind after space missions, like Apollo and all those spacecraft from years ago? Well, yes, but not only that, all other kinds of debris that we&#x27;ve dumped in space</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[15:59]</span> too. Anything from dead satellites to loose metal screws, there are even tiny particles of paint in liquid coolant. You now have some time to look at questions 24 to 26.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[16:26]</span> Now listen to the next part of the discussion and answer questions 24 to 26. So who is to blame for depositing all this rubbish? Where does it come from? Well, I knew you were going to ask me that, Sam.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[16:43]</span> So hang on, you can take some of my notes if you like. Thanks a lot. That&#x27;s really helpful. Here, look, over a third, 37 per cent to be exact, comes from Russia.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[16:56]</span> But other countries are close behind. Another third, well, just under actually 29 per cent is from America and then 28 per cent is from China. Yes, but other countries like India are adding to the rubbish pile.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[17:11]</span> And don&#x27;t forget the European Space Agency also has spacecraft in orbit. That&#x27;s true. We&#x27;re talking serious space junkies. Mm, pretty serious, I&#x27;d say.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[17:21]</span> So come on, what do you think are the chances of something solid dropping from space onto our heads? Good question, everyone asked that. Dr Chadwick said at least one piece of junk falls to earth every single day.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[17:37]</span> But look at it this way, earth is a pretty big place. So actually, the statistical chances of being hit are extremely low. You now have some time to look at questions 27 to 30. Now listen to the rest of the discussion and answer questions 27 to 30.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[18:23]</span> So are you saying I&#x27;m more likely to win the lottery? Well, just think about it. Two thirds of the earth is ocean. That&#x27;s true.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[18:32]</span> But in time, almost all these pieces of rubbish will fall to earth because the object&#x27;s orbit is decreased by its gravitational pull. But the good news is that they don&#x27;t cause any serious damage. You know, they can&#x27;t actually survive the heat generated on reentry.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[18:50]</span> They simply burn away. But that&#x27;s not always the case. There are exceptions. Chunks of the United States U.A.R.S. satellite recently fell into the Pacific Ocean.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[19:00]</span> The U.A.R.S. satellite. It was this six-tonne satellite launched by the Space Shuttle Discovery way back in 1991. So it had been up in space for 20 years but stopped working in 2005. It weighed 5,700 kilos.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[19:17]</span> And that&#x27;s about the same as a double-decker bus apparently. And just check my notes. Here it is. Yes.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[19:26]</span> The largest of these great big chunks that fell into the sea weighed about 158 kilograms. Think of the weight of an adult gorilla, Sam. And you get the picture. A nice soft landing, then.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[19:38]</span> Dr Chadwick said, imagine a couple of washing machines tied together and traveling at 100 miles per hour and you&#x27;ll get an idea. And do you remember Skylab? That was another U.S. space station.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[19:53]</span> And it fell to Earth at least three decades ago in 1979. It fell into the Indian Ocean and the deserts of Western Australia. According to what I wrote down, that particular space junk weighed 100 tons. And let&#x27;s not forget Mere, the Russian space station.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[20:14]</span> Mere weighed 135 tons, far, far larger than U.A.R.S. and it fell to Earth in 2001. It plunged straight into the South Pacific. All very interesting. Listen, I&#x27;ve got some junk of my own to sort out.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[20:31]</span> My bike. That&#x27;s a second puncture this week. Come on. I&#x27;ll help you fix it.</p><p><span class=\"ts\">[20:41]</span> This is the end of section three. You now have half a minute to check your answers. Track eight.</p>"
